SECURITY.md:36:**External tool delegation**: When Codex or Cursor are available, larch delegates tasks to them. For **review, sketches, and dialectic** tasks routed through `scripts/launch-review.sh --tool cursor` and `scripts/launch-review.sh --tool codex`, Codex and Cursor are now mechanically sandboxed at the CLI level (issue #1529): Codex review runs as `codex exec --sandbox read-only` and Cursor review runs as `cursor agent -p --trust --mode plan` (issue #1583 removed `--sandbox enabled`, which crashed hosts where the cursor-agent sandbox runtime is unavailable — the prior `codex exec --full-auto` (workspace-write) and `cursor agent -p --force --trust` (force-allow commands) postures are gone). `/design` plan-review voting uses `scripts/dispatch-plan-voters.sh`, which launches external voters through `scripts/run-external-agent.sh` directly but keeps the same read-only CLI posture (`codex exec --sandbox read-only` and `cursor agent -p --trust --mode plan`) and waits on wrapper sentinels before returning voter paths. The review launchers also provide a HARD CONSTRAINTS read-only preamble so the model reasons about its read-only role: Cursor prepends it to the prompt, while Codex writes it to a per-invocation `CODEX_HOME/config.toml` top-level `instructions` key outside both `$PWD` and the review output `--add-dir`. The plan-voter dispatcher includes a narrower do-not-modify voter prompt but does not publish launcher-owned dirty-tree sidecars; its primary mechanical controls are the external CLI read-only modes plus the monitored wrapper. For Codex, the CLI sandbox is the primary mitigation and the instructions preamble is its prompt-level reinforcement; for Cursor, read-only enforcement relies on `--mode plan` plus the prompt preamble plus the post-hoc `${OUTPUT}.dirty-tree` detector on review-launcher lanes (Cursor/Codex parity). Mechanical guarantee depends on the upstream Codex / Cursor CLIs continuing to honor `--sandbox read-only` / `--mode plan` respectively; the launcher harnesses (`scripts/test-launch-review.sh`) and plan-voter dispatcher harness (`scripts/test-dispatch-plan-voters.sh`) pin the argv literally, and review-launcher dirty-tree sidecars catch post-run mutation if a future CLI release silently changes those flags' semantics. Note that this hardening applies only to the **review-launcher and plan-voter lanes** — the implementer launchers (`scripts/launch-codex-implement.sh`, `scripts/launch-cursor-implement.sh`) intentionally retain workspace-write / `--trust` because the implementer's job is to edit the tree (see "Step 2 implementation" paragraph below). `/design` adaptive sketch process counts are projected in [`docs/topology.md`](docs/topology.md#design.sketch.regular_slots): full sketch mode launches 4 external agents, quick/simple sketch mode launches 2, and the codebase-scan-confirmed trivial-doc path launches 0 external sketch agents and writes sentinel artifacts instead. The trust model for nonzero sketch modes is unchanged — same scripts, same prompt constraints. `/implement` resolves the omitted-`--coder` implementer in **Step 1** (`### Implementer waterfall` in `skills/implement/SKILL.md`) before Step 2 dispatch; Step 2 implementation then consumes that resolved `--coder` and, when `--coder` was still omitted upstream, routes by external availability: Cursor → Codex → Claude (main agent only when both are unavailable); an explicit `--coder=cursor` still runs Cursor as `cursor agent -p --force --trust`, while the Codex fallback path uses `codex exec --full-auto` with `approval: never` and `sandbox: workspace-write`. The Codex implementer launcher (`scripts/launch-codex-implement.sh`) writes the static implementer agent preamble to a per-invocation `CODEX_HOME/config.toml` top-level `instructions` key, symlinks `~/.codex/auth.json` into that temporary home only when the file exists, and keeps the temporary home outside both `$PWD` and the dispatcher session tmpdir. The launcher passes two `--add-dir` flags to `codex exec`: (1) `--add-dir "$(dirname "$MANIFEST_PATH")"` so Codex's `workspace-write` sandbox can atomic-write `manifest.json` and `qa-pending.json` to the dispatcher-owned session tmpdir (outside `$PWD`, typically under `~/.cache/larch/sessions/...`); and (2) `--add-dir "$PWD"` to explicitly grant write access to the repo root — without this second grant the `workspace-write` sandbox denies writes to repo-root files (`Makefile`, `AGENTS.md`, etc.) and paths under `.claude/`, producing `sandbox-denied-required-root-files` bails. Together these widen Codex's writable surface to the repo root (and all paths beneath it) plus the entire session tmpdir. Operators should treat the session tmpdir as Codex-writable (transcripts, sidecars, dispatcher baseline / spawn / resume-count sentinels), but it does not contain Codex's temporary config or auth symlink (those live under `$CODEX_HOME_DIR`). Cursor (`--trust`) already permits writes to arbitrary absolute paths and requires no analogous launcher flag. The operator can opt into Cursor (`cursor agent ... --workspace "$PWD"`) with `--coder=cursor`, or the main Claude agent's Edit/Write path with `--coder=claude`. **Codex and Cursor do not commit** — Codex cannot write `.git/` under `workspace-write`, and Cursor prompts forbid commits. Cursor has full filesystem access, so the dispatcher (`skills/implement/scripts/step2-implement.sh`) mechanically asserts `HEAD == BASELINE_SHA` before committing on its behalf and bails with `cursor-modified-history` if the tool created or amended commits, preserving partial work for operator inspection. Operators who want a stricter sandbox model should prefer `--coder=codex`. The dispatcher pipes `manifest.commit_message` through `scripts/redact-secrets.sh`, writes the redacted output to a tmpfile, and runs `git add -A && git commit -F <tmpfile>` on the implementer's behalf — so what lands in git history is the manifest's `commit_message` modulo the redactor (the same scrubber used downstream for the canonical on-disk manifest). The redactor pass before `git commit` is load-bearing: without it, a secret accidentally embedded in `commit_message` would land permanently in git history while the on-disk canonical manifest's redacted copy would suggest the secret had been scrubbed (split brain). `git add -A` stages every working-tree change (tracked + untracked) — under this trust model the working tree IS the source of truth, `manifest.files_touched` is advisory documentation only (no diff cross-check), and operator review + `/review` (Step 5) + the regular pre-commit hook chain are the downstream backstops against external implementers committing files outside the declared scope. Before staging, the dispatcher compares `git status --porcelain` paths against manifest-declared paths and appends a best-effort Warning to `execution-issues.md` for undeclared working-tree paths; this makes scope drift visible but does not block the commit. Operators concerned about scope drift should review the produced commit before allowing the merge. There is no Claude-side diff verification — the working tree IS the source of truth, and `commit_message` flows into `git commit -F` modulo the secrets-family redaction described above. External implementer process writes bypass the Edit/Write PreToolUse hook chain (including `block-submodule-edit.sh`); the regular `pre-commit` hook chain runs when the dispatcher commits, providing a second-line backstop. Post-implementer validation in `skills/implement/scripts/step2-implement.sh` mechanically enforces, BEFORE the dispatcher commits: (1) `git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD` matches the spawn-time branch (→ `branch-changed`); (2) `git hash-object .claude-plugin/plugin.json` matches the spawn-time baseline, catching working-tree edits to the version-bump-reserved file (→ `protected-path-modified`); (3) `git submodule status --recursive` reports clean (→ `submodule-dirty`); (4) every manifest path is rejected if it contains `..`, starts with `/`, equals `.claude-plugin/plugin.json`, or resolves under a submodule (→ `protected-path-modified`); (5) Cursor only: `HEAD` still matches the spawn-time baseline (→ `cursor-modified-history`). The earlier diff cross-check (`manifest-diff-mismatch`), commit-subject equality (`commit-subject-mismatch`), working-tree-clean (`dirty-tree-after-codex`), and commits-since-baseline (`no-commit-since-baseline`) checks were removed when commit responsibility moved off Codex — they were tautological under the new model, since the dispatcher itself produces the commit from `manifest.commit_message`. If `git commit` fails (empty working tree, pre-commit hook rejection, transient git error), the dispatcher emits `commit-failed`, removes the un-sanitized `manifest.json` from `$IMPLEMENT_TMPDIR` to avoid leaving raw text fields on disk, captures `git commit` stderr to `$IMPLEMENT_TMPDIR/<tool>-commit-stderr.txt`, and bails — the index stays fully staged from the prior `git add -A` (no new commit was produced) so operators may need `git reset` or `git commit --amend` after diagnosing the failure, not only inspection. Manifest text fields (`commit_message`, `summary_bullets[]`, `todos_left[]`, `oos_observations[*].title|description`) are run through `scripts/redact-secrets.sh` before any consumption by downstream Steps 4 / 8a / 9a / 9a.1, since they flow verbatim into commit messages, the CHANGELOG, the PR body, and public GitHub issues. Claude does NOT inspect implementer transcripts or run `git diff` to figure out what changed — only the validated, sanitized manifest is consumed downstream. For `/research` — which launches external agents directly against `$PWD` — the trust framing (mechanical PreToolUse hook on `/research`'s `Edit|Write|NotebookEdit` surface, prompt-only posture for Bash and external agents, residual risks, and scope of the mechanical guarantee) is documented in the [External reviewer write surface in /research](#external-reviewer-write-surface-in-research) subsection below.
SECURITY.md:98:`.gitleaks.toml` maintains a narrow path-based allowlist: the config's self-allowlist (`^\.gitleaks\.toml$`), redaction-scanner source (`scripts/redact-secrets.sh`), redaction-scanner test fixtures (`scripts/test-redact-secrets.sh`), the tracking-issue-write lifecycle shell helper plus its contract doc and regression harness (`scripts/tracking-issue-write.sh`, `scripts/tracking-issue-write.md`, `scripts/test-tracking-issue-write.md`), and the committed run-log tree (`^larch-logs/`). The first four paths legitimately carry token-shaped strings throughout — regex literals, token-family tables, and synthetic test inputs — so per-line allowlisting would churn without adding signal. The `larch-logs/` allowlist suppresses `generic-api-key` false positives on UUID-shaped `LARCH_TOKEN_SESSION_ID` values that appear in committed session transcripts; **this means gitleaks Layers 1–2 do NOT scan files under `larch-logs/` for any pattern** — contributors must not paste live credentials, API keys, or other secrets into run-log files and must rely on the redaction pipeline (`scripts/redact-secrets.sh`, applied by `larch-log.sh` before each write) as the primary defence for that subtree. **High-churn documentation is NOT allowlisted** (#375): `README.md`, `CHANGELOG.md`, `SECURITY.md` itself, `skills/issue/SKILL.md`, and `skills/issue/scripts/create-one.sh` are scanned by gitleaks in both Layer 1 (pre-commit, working tree) and Layer 2 (CI, full history). The layer responsibilities remain distinct: gitleaks Layers 1–2 catch regex-matched patterns including synthetic token-shaped literals in docs; trufflehog Layer 3 (`--only-verified`) catches ONLY live, authenticable credentials and is non-redundant with gitleaks for that reason, NOT a replacement for it — an accidental paste of a revoked token or a covered-family token in an unusual format is caught by gitleaks Layers 1–2, not by the verified-only scan. Tokens whose format falls outside gitleaks' covered rule families (see the "Outbound shell-layer redaction" subsection below for the covered families) may slip both Layer 1–2 (no matching regex rule) and Layer 3 (nothing to authenticate against a live API) — contributors must not rely on scanner layers as a substitute for editorial discipline in docs. Contributors adding new token-shaped examples to docs should use non-detector-matching forms: short prefixes without the high-entropy suffix (e.g., `ghp_` as a prefix mention rather than a full 40-character token) or an explicit placeholder like `<REDACTED-TOKEN>`.
SECURITY.md:106:**Dev-only PostToolUse audit log (`scripts/audit-edit-write.sh`)**: `scripts/audit-edit-write.sh` is a contributor-local debugging aid that, when opted in via `.claude/settings.local.json`, records a JSONL trail of every `Edit` / `Write` tool invocation to `.claude/hook-audit.log`. The log is **intentionally unredacted** — it captures the raw PostToolUse payload including `tool_input` fields like `file_path`, `content`, and `old_string` / `new_string`, which may contain secrets, credentials, PII, or proprietary code. The file is **gitignored by default** (`.gitignore` lists `.claude/hook-audit.log`) and the script is **not registered in shipped config** (`hooks/hooks.json`) or in committed dev config (`.claude/settings.json`) — it runs only when a developer explicitly adds a `PostToolUse` entry to their local, gitignored `.claude/settings.local.json`. There is no automatic rotation or retention policy; the log grows until the developer clears it (`truncate -s 0 .claude/hook-audit.log`). Operational discipline: never commit the log, never paste its contents into issues/PRs/screenshots, clear it after debugging, and treat it as a secret-bearing artifact if the project handles secrets. See `docs/dev-hook-audit.md` for enable/disable/privacy details. The script always exits 0 so it cannot block tool use, and the regression test (`scripts/test-audit-edit-write.sh`, wired into `make lint`) uses `CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR` override plus a tmpdir to verify behavior without touching the real log.
